    "text": "```python\ndef merge_intervals(intervals: list[list[int]]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Merge overlapping intervals in a list of [start, end] pairs and return the merged list sorted by start.\n\n    Args:\n        intervals: A list of [start, end] pairs.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of merged [start, end] pairs sorted by start.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n\n    # Sort the intervals by start\n    intervals.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n\n    merged = [intervals[0]]\n    for current in intervals[1:]:\n        # If the current interval overlaps with the last merged interval, merge them\n        if current[0] <= merged[-1][1]:\n            merged[-1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], current[1])\n        else:\n            # Otherwise, add the current interval to the list of merged intervals\n            merged.append(current)\n\n    return merged\n\n# Example calls\nprint(merge_intervals([[1, 3], [2, 6], [8, 10], [15, 18]]))  # [[1, 6], [8, 10], [15, 18]]\nprint(merge_intervals([[1, 4], [4, 5]]))  # [[1, 5]]\nprint(merge_intervals([[1, 3], [5, 7], [2, 4], [6, 8]]))  # [[1, 4], [5, 8]]\n```",
